## 1. Introduction
Pollution of water resources is one of the worldwide environmental challenges. The development of various industries (e.g., textiles, cos­ metics, food, and pharmaceutical) and population growth give a large number of waste materials discharged into water bodies [1–3]. The most common water pollutants are dyes [4], heavy metals [5], emerging contaminants [6], phenols [7], and phosphates [8]. Different contami­ nants cause potential risks to human health as well as the environment. The use of heavy metals for various application and anthropogenic ac­ tivity (e.g., mining, foundries, liquid fuel burning, leaching of metals, and smelting) lead to their release into water sources. Water containing heavy metals causes health problems like brain damage, cancer, and gastrointestinal disease [9]. Synthetic dyes are another category of water pollutants that are widely applied in the textile, printing, leather, pharmaceutical, paper, and food industries [10]. Organic dyes are toxic, non-degradable, bio-accumulative, recalcitrant, carcinogenic, and mutagenic. Therefore, dye(s) containing wastewater harm aquatic or­ ganisms and human health [11]. Hazardous impacts of synthetic dyes on human life include damage to the nervous, brain, liver, kidney, and reproductive system, as well as cancer [4]. Numerous techniques have been reported for pollutant removal, including adsorption [12], advanced oxidation process (AOP) [13], membrane filtration [14], coagulation [15], electrochemical [16], and biological [17]. Adsorption is the most effective process for removing pollutants because of its economic feasibility, high performance, simplicity, and reusability of adsorbent [18].
Hydrogels with 3D polymeric network structures can hold a signifi­ cant quantity of water because of their hydrophilic functional groups, such as -COOH, -OH, $-S\mathrm{O}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ , - ${\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{CONH{_{2}}}$ , and - ${\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ [19,20]. Hydrogels possess tunable properties, biocompatibility, flexibility, low toxicity, versatility, and good swelling behavior [21]. Hydrogels are classified according to their source, chain composition, configuration, nature of cross-linking, ionic charge, size, and response to stimuli [22]. Hydrogels have been utilized in numerous fields, such as drug delivery [23], food [24], tissue engineering [25], biomaterial [26], soft robotics [27], regenerative medicine [28], as well as wastewater treatment [29–31].
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are a type of organic-inorganic hybrid materials that possess high porosity, large surface area, tunable structure, and flexibility. They are composed of a metal or metal oxide center and organic ligands [32,33]. Their porosity distribution is nar­ row, which allows control of their performance for different applications [34]. Therefore, MOFs have been extensively used for various applica­ tions, including pollutant removal [35], hydrogen storage [36], fluoride removal [37], $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ capture [38], oxidative desulfurization [39], biomedical fields [40], and dust removal [41]. Recently, MOFs-based hydrogels have been applied for the removal of numerous pollutants (e.g., heavy metals and synthetic dyes) from aqueous media [42–44].
Various MOFs-based hydrogel (nano-)composites have been fabri­ cated and exhibited as promising adsorbent candidates for water treat­ ment, owing to their unique properties using taking advantage of both MOFs and hydrogel matrixes. Abundant articles on hydrogels and MOFs have been published, but then utilization of MOFs-based hydrogels has been neglected. However, due to their several advantages, such as high adsorption capacity, porosity, suitable mechanical properties, dura­ bility, and biodegradability features, they will certainly attract the attention of researchers soon.
This article aims to review recent progress in the adsorption process for removing different pollutants using MOF-based hydrogels. In addi­ tion, MOFs properties, classifications, and synthetic approaches, as well as properties and advantages of MOFs-based hydrogels in wastewater treatment via the adsorption process have been introduced. Finally, future perspectives associated with the challenges of MOFs-based hydrogels in adsorption technology are proposed and discussed.
## 2. Adsorption technology
Common technologies for the removal of pollutants include mem­ brane filtration [45], adsorption [46], ion exchange [47], photochem­ ical [48], coagulation [49], advanced oxidation process (AOP) [50], electrochemical [51], and biological [52]. The advantages and disad­ vantages of these methods are listed in Table 1. Amongst, the adsorption approach has been widely used to remove of numerous pollutants owing to its simplicity, high efficiency, low cost, and reusability features [53,54]. The adsorption process contains two main classes, physical and chemical. The difference between physical and chemical adsorption approaches is based on their interaction type. In physisorption, the adsorbate molecule interacts with the surface through physical in­ teractions, including hydrogen bonding, Van der Waals forces, hydro­ phobicity, polarity, dipole-dipole, static, and $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions [55]. While, in chemisorption, adsorbate interacts with the surface by chem­ ical bond formation through electron transfer [56].
The adsorption process involves the pollutant passing through the bed containing sorbent. Using mechanisms of physical, chemical, and or ion exchange, the sorbent maintains the pollutants. The adsorbent is saturated and needs to regeneration when used at a certain period [55]. Favorable factors for selecting an adsorbent include high adsorption capacity, facile operation process, regeneration potential, and low cost [57]. Various types of adsorbents such as graphene oxide (GO) [58], poly(amidoamine)/carbon nanotubes (PAMAM/CNTs) [59], NaX zeolite [60], MIL-100(Fe) [61], poly(acrylic acid)/laponite (PAAc/Lap) nanocomposite hydrogel [62], and 1,4-PA-SBA [63] have been reported for the removal of pollutants from aqueous media. Different hydrogels are available and abundant that can be used to remove pollutants from aqueous solution. Adsorbents with high performance and effective cost have been developed successfully. Although numerous adsorbents have been applied for pollutant removal, MOFs-based hydrogels as suitable adsorbents have received attention more recently, which will be dis­ cussed in the following [42].
## 3. MOFs
## 3.1. Naming and properties of MOFs
The most promising types of porous materials that have been considered in the last years are MOFs. These materials are composed of metal or metal clusters and organic linkers, which can be coordinated into three-dimensional lattices [64]. Although there are many series of MOFs, there is no certain standard for their classification and naming. The naming of MOFs is the basis of material structure, composition, function, and laboratory or institution. Classification of the MOFs fam­ ilies is listed in Table 2. For example, in MIL-n, Materials of Institute Lavoisier is abbreviated as MIL and the number “n” is the serial number. Linkers for MOFs must be chelates and deprotonated such as benzene dicarboxylic (BDC), benzenetricarboxylic acids (BTC), and 2-methylimi­ dazole so that these are widely used for the synthesis of MOFs [65]. As yet, more than thousands of different MOFs with various morphologies, porous structures, and molecular formulas have been synthesized with various metals and ligands [66].
These compounds have unique properties that have led to their development [67]. They have high porosity, high surface area, and diverse functionalities. Due to the tunable porosity and shape (micro to mesoporous scale), MOFs have received special attention [68]. Based on ligands, metal nodes, various compositions, properties of physical, and topology, MOFs are tunable. Properties of MOFs materials specified using metal centers, organic linkers, and synthesis approach. Also, they show significant physical and chemical properties for the different ap­ plications. Their properties can be improved using several approaches, such as ion exchange, changing organic ligands, post-synthetic linker, impregnating appropriate active materials, composites with proper materials, grafting active groups and so on [69]. Nevertheless, they have some drawbacks, including poor selectivity, low sensitivity, and low stability. To overcome these limitations, MOFs are modified by various chemical approaches or made into composites [70].
## 3.2. Synthesis of MOFs
Owing to their physicochemical and certain functional properties, MOFs are considered a group of porous materials. These compounds are produced from bridging organic ligands and metal and or metal clusters. Also, primary building blocks are a noticeable concept in MOF's struc­ ture and properties. However, synthetic approaches and many param­ eters, such as solvent type, reaction temperature, pH, time, and pressure should be recognized. Different synthetic methods of MOFs, including hydrothermal [94], electrochemical [95], diffusion [96], mechano­ chemical [97], microwave [98], and ultrasound [99] are introduced successfully.
In the diffusion method, different species are gradually transferred to the reaction medium. This strategy can be classified into two approaches as follows.
i) In the first method, a liquid solvent is diffused. Two layers with various densities, including the precipitant solvent and the product in a solvent, are formed. Therefore, they are separated using a layer of solvent. The precipitant solvent is gradually conveyed into the separate layer and crystals grow.   
ii) In the second method, reactants are diffused using physical barriers, containing two vials of different sizes. Moreover, to reduce the rate of diffusion and inhibit the precipitation of the bulk compound, gels can be used [100,101].
MOFs have been synthesized under hydro- or solvo-thermal condi­ tions by conventional electrical heating. This method is simple and most common and led to excellent results in crystallinity and morphology properties compared to other synthetic techniques. The hydrothermal method involves the mixing of precursors under pressure and temper­ ature conditions and crystallization [102,103].
Microwave-assisted synthesis approaches have been widely employed for the fast fabrication of porous materials under hydrother­ mal conditions. This method has the advantages of phase selectivity, fast crystallization, simple morphology control, and narrow particle size distribution. In this procedure, the temperature is raised by microwave equipment to generate nanosized crystals. In the microwave method, a mixture in an appropriate solvent is transferred to a Teflon-lined stain­ less autoclave and placed in the microwave oven, and heated for a suitable time at a certain temperature [104,105].
Electrochemical synthesis is an alternative method for preparing of new materials; it causes control of reactants activity using changing electrode voltage. MOFs are prepared on an industrial scale using the electrochemical method. Advantages of this technique include avoiding anions such as metal nitrates, low temperature compared to hydro­ thermal synthesis, and fast synthesis [106,107].
The mechanochemical method takes place in chemical trans­ formation using mechanical breakage of intramolecular bonds. Also, the mechanical force created chemical reactions and various physical phe­ nomena. In this technique, reactions can be accomplished at ambient temperature without solvent, which is particularly beneficial when organic solvents can be eschewed. The advantages of mechanochemical synthesis are low temperature, solvent-free, and short reaction time [108,109].
Sonochemistry is well-considered as a method for the synthesis of nanomaterials in which a reaction mixture is subjected to ultrasound radiation in the range of $20~\mathrm{kHz}$ to $10~\mathrm{MHz}$ . Acoustic cavitation phe­ nomena cause creation, growth, and bubbles collapse in aqueous media. Nanomaterials can be easily fabricated by the ultrasonic approach. Furthermore, this method has advantages, including fast reaction time, environmentally friendly, facile to use, low temperature, and energyefficient [110].
## 3.3. Application of MOFs
According to the superior physicochemical properties of MOFs, these materials have been widely employed in different fields, including drug delivery [111], catalysis [112], adsorption [113], gas storage [114], separation of chemicals [115], and optical sensing [116]. However, in this assay, we have focused on removing hazardous compounds (e.g., synthetic dyes, heavy metals, phosphates, antibiotics, and some organic compounds) using MOFs-based hydrogels (nano-)composites. MOFs are efficient adsorbents for water remediation owing to their high porosity and surface area. They have been successfully applied for the adsorption of heavy metals [117], dyes [118], phosphate [119], pharmaceuticals [120], and so on. High surface area and porosity caused facile diffusion into adsorbent sites. However, as mentioned previously, owing to some drawbacks of MOFs, such as intrinsic fragility, unsatisfied processability, dust formation, and poor reusability, these materials are often designed as supported-based MOFs (e.g., MOFs-coated composites) or 3D struc­ tured composites, such as MOFs-based hydrogels. Amongst, MOFs-based hydrogels will be discussed in the following.
## 4. MOFs-based hydrogels
Hydrogels are hydrophilic polymers with 3D networks that include various structures and chemical compositions. They have various ad­ vantages that cause different applications [121,122]. MOFs with 3D structures composed of metal ions clusters linked using organic ligands have attracted attention due to their various potential applications [123,124]. MOFs have high activity and are useful as reinforcing agents in hydrogels. Nevertheless, most powder MOFs have a rigid structure and low external hydrophilic, which causes weak flexibility, particle aggregation, limited biomedical, and practical applications. Moreover, powder MOFs are difficult to separate from aqueous solutions, so that limited their industrial applicability [53,125]. Hydrophilic polymers modified-MOFs increase hydrophilicity and prevent aggregation [126]. The MOFs-based hydrogels have both advantages of hydrogels and MOFs. Compared with other hydrogels, MOFs-based hydrogels possess enhanced durability, mechanical properties (owing to the rigid structure of MOFs), and high specific surface areas (due to the mesoporous structure of MOFs, as well as the swelling nature of hydrogels) [127–129]. Thus, the incorporation of MOFs particles into 3D networks that demonstrate different chemical compositions and structures has improved the practical efficiency of these crystalline materials. MOFsbased hydrogels are generally fabricated using incorporating MOF material into the hydrogel matrices. Likewise, the MOFs and hydrogel matrixes can be considered as dispersed and the continuous phases, respectively (Scheme 1) [130]. Besides, these materials can be easily shaped into different structures [131].
MOFs-based hydrogels have various applications, including adsorp­ tion [132], wound healing [133], sensing [134], supercapacitor [135], medical [136], energy storage [137], and catalysis [138]. MOFs-based hydrogels are promising candidates for the application in sorption technique due to their proper adsorption capacity, good mechanical properties, and durability [132].
## 5. The application of MOFs-based hydrogels for water treatment
Different types of pollutants are released from industrial effluent into the water environment. However, the use of water sources is unavoid­ able [139]. Thus, it is necessary to develop proper techniques to remove contaminants from water bodies. As the above-mentioned inherent properties of MOFs-based hydrogels, these materials have been exploi­ ted to remove of numerous pollutants (e.g., synthetic dyes, heavy metals, antibiotics, organic compounds, and phosphate) and exhibited favorable performance potential. The removal of the mentioned compounds using MOFs-based hydrogels will be discussed in the next sections. It should be pointed out that despite the superior physicochemical and biological features of MOFs-based hydrogels, these materials are under-exploited for water treatment. It is expected this assay will open a new opportu­ nity for more focus on the exploring of MOFs-based hydrogels in envi­ ronmental science.
## 5.1. Removal of synthetic dyes
As mentioned in the Introduction section, the removal of synthetic dyes from wastewater is necessary before discharging to water bodies owing to their non-biodegradable and toxic nature. In this context, MOFbased hydrogels have been introduced as a relatively new generation of adsorbent to remove synthetic dyes through the adsorption process. Some examples in the field are discussed in the following.
Ribeiro group [42] synthesized UIO-66, and then solvent-assisted ligand incorporation was applied to modify UIO-66 using acrylic acid (UIO-66@AA). A methacrylate Arabic gum (AGMA) was synthesized through the reaction between Arabic gum (AG) and glycidyl methac­ rylate (GMA). Afterward, a superabsorbent hybrid hydrogel composed of poly(sodium acrylate) (PSA), poly(acryl amide) (PAAm), AGMA, and UIO-66@AA was synthesized via a free radical polymerization approach (Scheme 2). The synthesized hybrid hydrogel showed the highest swelling ratio at pH 9 (ca. 305 times). The synthesized hydrogel was applied as an adsorbent to remove methylene blue (MB) dye from an aqueous solution. The hydrogel illustrated excellent efficiency for MB dye, so that highest adsorption amount was obtained as $768.0\dot{3}\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $\mathtt{p H7}$ . The kinetic and adsorption models were pseudo-second-order and Langmuir, respectively, for their experiments. Also, the adsorbent demonstrated excellent desorption/adsorption efficiencies for five cycles.
Liu and coworkers [140] developed an HKUST-1 (which is also called MOFs-199)-incorporated hydrogel composed of hydroxyethyl cellulose (HEC), acryl amide (AAm), and alginate (Alg) with tunable mechanical properties. HEC was added as a non-ionic shear-thinning agent to ach­ ieve direct ink writing (DIW) properties, and it was found that the inks with $4\mathrm{wt\%}$ HEC had the greatest printability. The hydrogel/MOFs were synthesized through three-dimensional (3D) printing approach. MOFs were dispersed in the double network (DN) hydrogel matrix to create composites with high porosity that could facilitate penetration of rhodamine 6G (Rh6G) and methylene blue (MB) dyes inside the hydrogel matrix. Hydrogel/HKUST-1 illustrated the ability for sizeselective absorption of dyes. Based on this fact, the smaller dye (MB, $7.0\mathring\mathrm{A})$ showed higher penetration $(96~\%)$ than those of the larger Rh6G dye (13.0 $\mathring\mathrm{A}$ ; $33.3~\%$ ).
In an interesting attempt, Klein et al. [141] reported the green syn­ thesis of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based MOFs/hydrogel composite. For this purpose, Alg was crosslinked by $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zr}}^{4+}$ to afford an “egg-box” cross-linked structure. Then, a series of hydrogel/MOFs were fabricated through the reaction of tri­ mesic acid with $\mathrm{Zr/Alg}$ under hydrothermal conditions at $50^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . During the reaction, some of the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zr}}^{4+}$ in the hydrogels self-assembled with the trimesic acid linker to produce a porous MOFs-like structures embedded within the hydrogel (Zr-MOFs-Alg composites). The MB removal efficiency of the Zr-MOFs-Alg composites was examined, and maximum adsorption capacity was obtained as $4.361\pm0.092\mathrm{{\mg\k^{-1}}}$ (of $\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\mathrm{Zr}}$ ) in an initial MB concentration of $9.6~\mathrm{mg~L}^{-1}$ at neutral pH.
Another interesting hydrogel/MOFs composed of poly(acryl amide)/ polyanionic cellulose/Zr (PAM/PAC/Zr MOFs) was synthesized by the Zheng group [142]. The adsorbent was prepared through acrylamide polymerization in a mixture of PAC and disodium terephthalate $\mathbf{(Na_{2}B D C)}$ , subsequently by post-treatment in zirconium oxychloride octahydrate $\mathrm{(ZrOCl_{2})}$ ) aqueous solution. The coordination of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zr}}^{4+}$ clus­ ters with carboxylates groups of PAC and disodium terephthalate $\mathbf{(Na_{2}B D C)}$ , improved the mechanical properties of hydrogels as well as enhanced the adsorption of methyl orange (MO) dye. Despite this, increasing the $\mathsf{B D C}^{2-}$ and PAC content in the sample led to a decrease in the swelling ratio. Based on Langmuir isotherm, the maximum adsorp­ tion capacity of the adsorbent for MO dye was obtained as $500~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ Zr.
Zhu et al., reported the development of a physical hydrogel-based adsorbent composed of HKUST-1 MOFs, poly(vinyl alcohol)-co-poly­ ethylene (PVA-co-PE) nanofibers, and PVA matrix. The hydrogel was synthesized through freeze-thaw for three cycles [143]. The fabricated hydrogel/MOFs were applied as adsorbent for the removal of cationic (methylene blue, rhodamine B, and methyl violet) and anionic (Argacid Turk. blue, reactive red, and reactive yellow) dyes. As results (Fig. 1), adding of MOFs to hydrogels slightly increased the adsorption capacities of all dyes. In addition, both HKUST-1 and HKUST-1 $@$ PVA-co-PE/PVA hybrid hydrogel showed so much higher affinity toward cationic dyes than those of the anionic dyes. The adsorption processes were well-fitted with the Langmuir isotherm and pseudo-second-order kinetic model.
In another attempt, Zhu et al., developed a novel approach for in situ growth of HKUST-1, ZIF-8, MIL-100(Fe), and ZIF-67 MOFs in the Alg matrix, which overcomes the challenges of processing MOFs into specially shaped structures for real industrial applications [144]. The ZIF-8-Alg and ZIF-67-Alg demonstrated high surface areas than HKUST1- and MIL-100-Alg samples. Results exhibited that metal ion crosslinked Alg hydrogels are efficient for the treatment of dyecontaminated water.
## 5.2. Removal of heavy metals
Heavy metals are another category of water pollutants that are dis­ charged directly or indirectly into water bodies by various industries (e. $g.$ , batteries, fertilizer industries, tanneries, paper industries, and pesti­ cides) and lead to serious problems owing to their non-biodegradable, toxic, and carcinogenic nature, as well as tends to accumulate in the food chain of organisms. The most important heavy metals in waste­ water are nickel (II), Cr (VI), mercury, lead (II), and Cu (II). Nickel exceeding led to numerous cancers, serious kidney and lung problems aside from gastrointestinal distress, skin dermatitis, and pulmonary fibrosis [145]. Exposure to Cr (VI) led to genetic defects and numerous cancers [146]. High mercury concentrations lead to damage to the central nervous system and impairment of pulmonary and kidney function, dyspnea, and chest pain. Cu is one of the mostly employed heavy metals in several industries, including electroplating, electrical, mining, and metallurgy. Exposure to this heavy metal can excite the eyes and nasal mucosa, as well as cause some symptoms, such as stomach pain, dizziness, and diarrhea. In addition, too much intake can lead to kidney and liver damage and even death. Lead (II) is another toxic heavy metal, which acutely poisons nerves, blood and blood vessels, the digestive system, the liver, the reproductive system, and kidneys when certain concentrations are reached, leading to several systemic organ diseases [145].
As the above discussion, the treatment of metal-polluted wastewater is vital before its discharge to the surrounding environment or water bodies. This process can be accomplished using numerous treatment approaches, including centrifugation, filtration or floatation, ion ex­ change, sedimentation, chemical precipitation, ultrafiltration, reverse osmosis, electrodialysis, coagulation/flocculation, and adsorption [147–150]. Amongst, adsorption technology has received more atten­ tion owing to its effective and economic properties, which are discussed extensively in the Introduction section. Numerous adsorbents such as activated carbon [151], carbon nanotubes [152], clay [153], zeolite [154], and SBA-15 [155] have been applied for the removal of heavy metals. In this context, MOFs-based hydrogels have been introduced as a new category of bio-sorbents for the removal of heavy metals. Natural polymers (e.g., Alg and gelatin) are the most exploited matrix for the synthesis of MOFs-based hydrogels toward the removal of heavy metals that will be discussed in the following.
In an interesting work, Li et al., reported in situ growth of ZIF-67 in Alg hydrogel and investigated its performance in the removal of Cu (II) from aqueous solutions [44]. The surface area and pore size of the fabricated adsorbent were $345.34~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $22.56\ \mathrm{nm}$ , respectively. The optimal condition for the adsorption process was obtained as $\mathtt{p H}5$ , and an adsorbent dosage of $10\mathrm{mg}$ . The fabricated Alg/ZIF-67 composite hydrogel exhibited higher removal efficiency of Cu (II) $(153.63\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ in comparison with ZIF-67 $\left(111.79\mathrm{~mg~g^{-1}}\right)$ . This may be originated from the interaction between hydroxyl and carboxyl groups of $\mathtt{A l g}$ with Cu (II) ions and the pours structure of ZIF-67. It was found that the fabricated adsorbent can be removed Cu (II) ions more efficiently in comparison with Cd (II), Ni (II), Mn (II), and $Z\mathbf{n}$ (II) that confirm the excellent selectivity of Alg/ZIF-67 composite hydrogel to Cu (II) ions (Fig. 2). Isotherm and kinetic studies revealed that the adsorption technique was well-fitted with Langmuir isotherm $(\mathrm{R}^{2}=0.9972)$ and pseudo-second-order models $(\mathrm{R}^{2}=0.9822)$ , respectively. In addition, the adsorbent exhibited good reusability for five adsorption-desorption cycles.
In a similar study, Zhang et al. [156] synthesized a PVA-Alg/ZIF-8 composite hydrogel through in situ growth of ZIF-8 into a polymeric solution of PVA-Alg. The developed composite hydrogel was used as an adsorbent for the removal of copper (II) from aqueous solutions. The PVA-Alg/ZIF-8-20 that was synthesized by adding of $20~\mathrm{mL}$ of 2-methyl­ imidazole showed the highest adsorption capacity (approximately 165 $\mathbf{mg\g}^{-1})$ . They optimized the adsorption condition as an adsorbent dosage 5 mg, pH 4, Cu (II) concentration $300\mathrm{\mg\L^{-1}}$ , and temperature $313\mathrm{~K~}$ According to the Langmuir isotherm model, the maximum adsorption capacity of the adsorbent for copper (II) obtained $166.94\mathrm{mg}$ ${\bf g}^{-1}$ at $298.15\mathrm{K}.$ . Kinetic model was well-fitted with pseudo-second-order model. The adsorbent exhibited excellent re-usability for five times (removal efficiency reduced only $10\%$ ). In addition, the developed PVAAlg/ZIF-8 composite hydrogel showed excellent selective adsorption toward copper (II) ions in comparison with Cd (II), Ni (II), Co (II), and Mn (II) ions (Fig. 3).
More recently, Mahmoud et al., developed pectin/MOFs composite hydrogel (PHM) for Cr (VI) and Pb (II) ions removal from aqueous media [43]. The synthetic procedure of composite is shown in Scheme 3. The pectin was derived from mandarin orange peel by-products and then crosslinked using $\mathsf{C a^{2+}}$ ions. Fe/tartaric acid MOFs were in situ incor­ porated into the ${\mathsf{C a}}^{2+}$ -crosslinked pectin. The synthesized PHM showed excellent physicochemical features, including high swelling capacity $(1500.0\%)$ after only $400\mathrm{{min}}$ , as well as good surface area $(149.55\mathrm{m}^{2}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}\big]$ ) and pore volume $(3.72\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})$ . Then, the developed composite hydrogel was applied for the removal of Cr (VI) and Pb (II) ions. The maximum adsorption amounts for these ions were obtained as 825.97 and $913.88~\mathrm{mg~g}^{-1}$ , respectively, under optimal conditions (composite dosage of $0.1\mathrm{g},\mathrm{pH}7\$ , initial concentration $100\mathrm{mgL}^{-1}$ , and contact time $120~\mathrm{{min}}$ at $55^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The PHM exhibited excellent reusability up to eight cycles without significant loss of removal efficiency, especially for Cr (VI). The adsorption kinetics studies showed that Cr (VI)/Pb (II) ions removal was described via the pseudo-second-order model, and the adsorption data were well-fitted by Langmuir isotherm.
Jin et al., fabricated carboxymethylcellulose (CMC)-coated MOFs-5 and CMC beads through crosslinking with $\mathbb{A}^{3+}$ ions for removal of Pb (II) from aqueous solutions [157]. The developed CMC/MOFs-5 adsor­ bent showed significantly higher Pb (II) adsorption capacity $(132.04\mathrm{mg}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1})$ ) than those of the MOF-5 $\cdot117.67\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\;$ ) as well as CMC $\mathrm{108.8mg}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1})$ . As a result, CMC/MOFs-5 adsorbent can be re-used for four cycles. Optimal condition was obtained as contact time $120~\mathrm{min}$ , $\mathtt{p H}6$ , and adsorbent dosage $45~\mathrm{mg}$ . The maximum adsorption capacity for CMC/ MOFs-5 beads was obtained as $322.58~\mathrm{~mg~}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . The adsorption isotherm and kinetic studies revealed that the sorption process is well followed with the Langmuir isotherm and pseudo-second-order model, respectively. Also, thermodynamics studies show that the adsorption process is spontaneous and endothermic.
More recently, Yang et al. [158] fabricated gelatin/UiO- $66\substack{-\textnormal{N O}_{2}}$ composite hydrogel through a simple thermal-treatment self-rearranged technique as an adsorbent for the removal of Pb (II) ions from aqueous media. In this process, carboxylic acid groups in the UiO-66- ${\cdot\mathrm{NO}_{2}}$ chemically reacted with amine groups in the gelatin backbone to establish stable composites. The selective adsorption of $\mathrm{\bf{Pb}}$ (II) using gelatin/UiO-66- ${\cdot\mathrm{NO}_{2}}$ composite hydrogel was tested in the presence of $\mathsf{C a}^{2+}$ and $\mathbf{Mg}^{2+}$ as the most common metal ions in water. It was found that the removal performance of $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ reduced with the increasing of coexisted ${\mathsf{C a}}^{2+}$ and $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ concentrations, and interestingly that the ef­ fect of coexisted $\mathsf{C a}^{2+}$ is more than the coexisted $\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}$ on $\dot{\mathrm{Pb}}^{2+}$ removal. It should be pointed out that the removal performances of $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ were obtained over $80\%$ where both the molar ratio values of Ca (II) and $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}$ (II) with Pb (II) are up to 4, which confirms the potential of gelatin/UiO$66\substack{-\textnormal{N O}_{2}}$ composite hydrogel for selective removal of $\mathrm{\sfPb}$ (II). The maximum adsorption amount for $\mathbf{Pb^{2+}}$ was achieved as $529.52\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $45~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The adsorption process is described with pseudo-second-order and the Langmuir models. The regeneration studies revealed that the developed gelatin/UiO- $66\substack{-\textnormal{N O}_{2}}$ composite hydrogel could be used up to eight times without substantial loss in its removal performance.
Caldera-Villalobos et al. [159] employed biopolymeric-based com­ posite hydrogels obtained from collagen and guar gum (Col/GG) to remove water pollutants, including ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Ni^{2+}}$ , and $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ ions as well as mordant brown, direct red, and basic pink dyes. Degradation studies in various hydrolytic and proteolytic media (basic; $\mathrm{pH}12\$ , acidic; $\mathsf{p H}2$ , neutral; $\mathrm{pH}7.4$ , papain and collagenase; 14 units/hydrogel) revealed that the incorporation of MgMOFs-74, CaMOFs-74, and $\mathbf{Zn}(\mathbf{Atz})(\mathbf{Py})$ MOFs into Col/GG semi-IPN composite hydrogels lead to substantially decrease of biodegradability behavior. The content of MgMOFs-74, CaMOFs-74, and $\mathbf{Zn}(\mathbf{Atz})(\mathbf{Py})$ MOFs into the biopolymers-based hydro­ gels were set at $1\mathrm{wt\%}$ (Scheme 4). These composite hydrogels represent proper adsorption performance for the removal of ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ and ${\mathrm{Zn}}^{{\bar{2}}+}$ ions. Amongst these, the Col/GG/MgMOFs-74 composite hydrogel showed the highest removal performance. In the case of mentioned dyes, excellent removal was obtained for direct red dye using $\mathrm{Col/GG/}$ CaMOFs-74 composite hydrogel in neutral pH. Finally, re-usability studies results exhibited that the hydrogels conserved the removal per­ formance eight times, and after that, a decrease in the adsorption amount was revealed for all samples.
Liu et al. [160] synthesized $\mathrm{ZrO_{2}}$ -based MOFs-808/amidoxime (AO) composite hydrogel via a sol-gel method. AO functional groups were grafted to the MOFs-808 surface via a wet chemistry approach. The specific surface area for MOFs-808 and MOFs-808/AO were obtained as 2152 and $1899{\mathrm{~m}}^{2}{\mathrm{~g}}^{-1}$ , respectively. After functionalization of MOFs808 using AO, the average pore sizes were decreased from 17.1 to $13.6\ \mathrm{nm}$ . These decreased values could be related to introducing AO functional groups, which blocked some holes on the MOFs-808 surface. The $\mathrm{Hg}$ (II) adsorption performance of MOFs-808 and MOFs-808/AO was examined in aqueous media. The optimal condition was obtained as $\mathrm{pH}5.5$ , $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , contact time $70\mathrm{min}$ , and initial $\mathrm{Hg}$ (II) concentration $600\mathrm{~mg~L^{-1}}$ . The results indicated that the adsorption amount of MOFs$808/\mathrm{AO}$ $(383.8~\mathrm{mg~g^{-1}})\$ was higher than MOFs-808 $(343.6~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ . Adsorption isotherm and kinetic models were well-fitted with the
Langmuir isotherm and pseudo-second-order models, respectively.
## 5.3. Removal of antibiotics
As a decisive fact, antibiotics have an essential role in human health. The development and production of antibiotics have progressively increased after the discovery of penicillin and streptomycin [161,162]. As the World Health Organization (WHO), the American Public Health Association (APHA), and the American Medical Association (AMA) warn, the progressive use of antibiotics can lead to potential health problems in humans, as well as the environment, and ecological systems. The most common side effects of antibiotics can be listed as nausea, diarrhea and stomach pain, dehydration as well as antibiotic resistance [162]. Therefore, the removal of antibiotic residue from pharmaceutical companies and hospital wastewater has attracted great interest. In this context, MOFs-based hydrogels have been suggested as novel and effi­ cient adsorbents for the removal of residual antibiotics that will be discussed in the following.
In 2019, Zhuang et al. [163] evaluated alginate and Co (II)-based ZIF-67 composite hydrogels for tetracycline removal from aqueous so­ lution. The samples were synthesized by a one-step method. The MA-M sample that was synthesized by adding Co (II) and 2-methylimidazolate in methanol to Alg solution showed the best tetracycline removal per­ formance. The optimal pH for the adsorption process was to be 2. Ac­ cording to the Langmuir equation, the maximum adsorption capacity for ZIF-67-alginate hydrogel (AM-M) and ZIF-67-2-methylimidazolate hydrogel (MA-M) achieved 302.32 and $364.89\mathrm{~mg~g}^{-1}$ , respectively. In regeneration study, the MA-M sample revealed that this product could be used for 10 cycles with acceptable removal performance. The microstructure of the materials with a similar rigid structure indicated the MA-M has a whole MOFs structure, while the AM-M was a polymer skeleton.
More recently, Chai et al. [164] fabricated ZIF-8/polysaccharide (sodium alginate-kappa-carrageenan, SC) hydrogel beads, denoted as ZIF-8/SC, through a one-pot process (self-assembly and cross-linking process simultaneously) for ciprofloxacin (CIP) removal from aqueous solution. The developed ZIF-8/SC hydrogel displayed an excellent adsorption capacity of $2887~\mathrm{mg~g^{-1}}$ in optimal condition (neutral $\mathsf{p H}$ , temperature $=298.15$ K, CIP concentration $=200~\mathrm{mg~L}^{-1}$ , adsorbent $\mathtt{l o s a g e}=0.168\mathtt{g}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ , contact time $=10\mathrm{h}$ ). It was found that the ZIF-8/ SC composite hydrogel beads had higher uptake toward CIP $(951.61\pm$ $33.74\mathrm{~mg~g^{-1})}$ in comparison with the ZIF-8/SC blended beads (608.82 $\pm22.86\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\mathrm{\:;}$ and/or the ZIF-8/SC composite beads that synthesized in two-step method $(318.54~\pm~11.46~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ . They suggested that electrostatic and $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions, hydrogen bonding, and coordination effects are the most important reasons for the adsorption of CIP onto/ into the ZIF-8/SC composite. Also, adsorption and kinetic models were best fitted with the Langmuir isotherm and pseudo-second-order, respectively.
Removal of ofloxacin using Alg/ZIF-8 composite gel through an adsorption process was investigated by Yu and Wu [165]. The composite was synthesized via in situ growth of ZIF-8 into Alg gel which led to coarse wrinkles with many pores inside. The optimum condition for the removal of ofloxacin using Alg/ZIF-8 composite gel was obtained as ofloxacin initial concentration $100\mathrm{mgL}^{-1}$ , adsorbent dosage $10\mathrm{mg}$ , pH
9, temperature 308 K. Result showed that Alg/ZIF-8 composite could remove $97.7\pm0.9\ \%$ of ofloxacin so that the equilibrium adsorption capacity achieved $160.6\pm1.3\mathrm{{\mg\g}^{-1}}$ . In addition, the adsorption performance can maintain $85.5~\%$ after four adsorption-desorption cy­ cles. Finally, the kinetic and adsorption isotherm studies displayed that the adsorption process was best fitted with pseudo-second-order and Langmuir models, respectively.
More recently, Cheng et al. [166] investigated the removal of tetracycline (TC) as well as Co (II), MB, and CR dyes from wastewater by University of Oslo-66 (UiO-66) MOFs/Alg hydrogels. The UiO-66 is modified by various functional groups (e.g., $\mathrm{-NH_{2}}$ and $\scriptstyle-\mathrm{NO}_{2},$ . Amongst these, $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N O}_{2}$ showed higher removal performance than UiO-66- $\mathrm{{NH}_{2}}$ and UiO-66 in the case of TC and CR dyes. In addition, UiO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NO}_{2}/$ Alg exhibited an excellent regeneration capacity than those of the two others. In detail, UiO-66- ${\bf\cdot N O}_{2}$ -based hydrogel exhibited an adsorption capacity of $71.56~\mathrm{mg~g^{-1}}$ for TC and suitable regeneration $(85~\%)$ after five times. The adsorption process was described by the Langmuir isotherm and pseudo-second-order kinetic models.
The biochar/MOFs derivative covered with κ-carrageenan/calcium alginate hydrogel (BC/HDC-550/κ-car/CA) was successfully fabricated and used to remove TC antibiotics from wastewater by the $\mathtt{Y u}$ group [167]. The maximum adsorption capacity for TC was obtained as $396.09\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . Also, in a continuous system, a novel hydrogel showed effective performance in the removal of organic pollutants. Adsorption mechanisms were hydrogen bonding and electrostatic.
## 5.4. Removal of organic compounds
As industrialization and society growth, the production of waste­ water containing numerous organic compounds increased significantly, which caused critical problems for human health and the environment owing to their toxic natures in most cases. Therefore, their removal from wastewater is necessary from environmental and health points of view.
Humic acids (HAs) are high-molecular-weight organic compounds that contain aromatic rings and phenolic hydroxyl and carboxyl func­ tionalities. These functional groups act as binding sites for heavy metal ions, including iron, zinc, lead, and cadmium that lead to toxicity for humans and animals [168]. Therefore, removing HA from water is necessary to avoid the mentioned toxicity. In this context, Maan et al. [169] found that poly(acrylamide) (PAAm)/ZIF-8 hydrogel could act as an ideal adsorbent for HA. In this work, zinc hydroxide/PAAm com­ posite was used as a precursor gel for the incorporating of ZIF-8 in a PAAm hydrogel. The pore size of this adsorbent was obtained to be 5.7 $\pm\ 0.5\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The highest swelling ratio for PAAm/ZIF-8 hydrogel was obtained about $11.7\pm0.1$ times in basic conditions $\mathrm{(pH~10)}$ . The maximum adsorption amount was $111.5\pm3.0\mathrm{\mg\}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . The optimum conditions were a contact time of 7 days and an initial concentration of HA as $150\mathrm{~mg~L^{-1}}$ . The adsorption model was described by the Hill equation.
Bisphenol A (BPA) is an aromatic compound that is used in numerous industries, including epoxy resins and polycarbonate plastic production, thermal paper, as well as food packaging [170]. BPA has estrogen-like and anti-androgen effects that damage different tissues, including im­ mune, reproductive, and neuroendocrine systems. It has been found that this organic compound could induce mutagenesis and carcinogenesis in animal models even at low exposure levels [171]. As these facts, BPA has been controversial for quite some time and has received intense scrutiny from government agencies, environmental groups, and the media. Therefore, the removal of BPA from aqueous media has attracted a great deal of interest.
In this context, Luo et al. [172] synthesized sodium alginatechitosan/aluminum-based MOFs (SA-CS/Al-MOFs) composite beads as adsorbents for the removal of BPA from aqueous solution. It was found that the addition of CS changed the pore structure of the supports and the achieved Al-MOFs/SA-CS adsorbent had a higher surface area and total pore volume than the supports without CS. The SA-CS-3/Al-MOFs sample illustrates a surface area of $687.54\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , a pore volume of 0.61 $\mathsf{c m}^{3}\mathsf{\bar{g}}^{-1}$ with a micropore volume of $0.19\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . They achieved a maximum adsorption capacity of $136.9\ \mathrm{mg\g^{-1}}$ under optimum condi­ tions using an initial concentration of BPA 120 ppm, $\mathtt{p H}6$ , and reaction time $^{18\mathrm{{h}}}$ at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ using the SA-CS-3/Al-MOFs sample. They concluded that $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions, hydrogen and cation- $\scriptstyle\cdot\pi$ bonding are the most important physical interactions that affect the adsorption of BPA (Scheme 6).
## 5.5. Removal of phosphate
In the last decades, many industrial companies and sewage have discharged phosphor-enriched wastewater into the water bodies or the environment, leading to numerous problems, including frequent oc­ currences of oxygen deficit (owing to the rapid growth of algae), fish kills, algal blooms, and various bacterial infections. As requirements of the Environment Protection Agency (EPA) for phosphate, the highest permitted quantity and stringent discharge limit are 0.1 and $0.05~\mathrm{mg}$ $\bar{\mathbf{L}}^{-1}$ , respectively. Therefore, the complete removal or reduce amount of phosphate from wastewater has received a great deal of interest [173,174].
More recently, Wang et al. [175] developed an Alg/Fe-filled MOFsAl-based hydrogel (Alg@Fe@MOFs-Al) via in situ growth of MOFs within Alg matrix followed by crosslinking with ${\mathsf{C a}}^{2+}$ ions for removal of phosphate form aqueous solutions. The point of zero charge $\mathrm{(pH_{pzc})}$ for the sample was obtained to be 4.7. After sorption of $\mathrm{H_{2}P O^{4-}}$ ions, $\mathrm{\pH_{\mathrm{pzc}}}$ of the ${\mathrm{Alg/Fe}}$ -filled MOFs-Al-based hydrogel was increased to 6.3, owing to electrostatic interaction between $\mathrm{H_{2}P O^{4-}}$ ions and carboxylate groups in the Alg backbone. The optimal condition was obtained as initial $\mathrm{H_{2}P O^{4-}}$ concentration $25~\mathrm{mg~L}^{-1}$ , adsorbent dosage $\boldsymbol{1}\ \mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{L}^{-1}$ , contact time $20\mathrm{min}$ , and temperature $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The maximum adsorption amount for the biosorbent was calculated as $103.09\ \mathrm{~mg~}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ . Adsorption isotherm and kinetic studies revealed that the sorption process is more fitted with Langmuir and pseudo-first-order models, respectively. Thermodynamic results exhibited that the adsorption process is exothermic and spontaneous.
In another study, Zhang group [176] applied MIL-101 (Al/Fe) MOFsincorporated chitosan (CS) for phosphate removal from an aqueous so­ lution. The result demonstrated that this hydrogel has an excellent performance in phosphate removal $(49.8\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1}) $ . Adsorption isotherm and kinetic studies revealed that the sorption process is more fitted with Langmuir and pseudo-second-order models, respectively. Thermody­ namics studies results ( $.\Delta G<0$ , $\Delta\mathrm{H}>0$ , and $\Delta s>0\AA$ , revealed that phosphate adsorption onto/into MOFs-CS is endothermic, spontaneous, and disordered. Re-usability studies exhibited that the MOFs-CS adsor­ bents can preserve $92\%$ of the initial sorption capacity's after ten cycles.
Agriculture is a major factor in phosphorus pollution owing to the overuse of P-containing fertilizers. More recently, Wang et al. [177] synthesized poly(N-isopropyl acrylamide-co-acrylamide)-coated AlgBMOFs (PABMOFs) for phosphate adsorption from aqueous solution. For this purpose, BMOFs were synthesized through the reaction between $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}$ , $\mathrm{Mg(CH_{3}C O O)_{2}},$ and 2,5-dihydroxy terephthalic in water/ethanol/ DMF mixture as the solvents under autoclave condition at $125^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 24 h. Afterward, Alg/BMOFs was synthesized through the mixing of Alg and BMOFs in water followed by crosslinking with hexamethylene dii­ socyanate at $70~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . Finally, the PABMOFs were fabricated via grafting and simultaneous crosslinking of $N$ -isopropyl acrylamide and acryl­ amide monomers in ionic liquids microemulsion $\mathrm{\langleBmim]PF6/TX-100/}$ water) as nano-reactor through a free radical polymerization approach (Scheme 7). The surface area and pore size of PABMOFs were achieved as $42.36~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and ${396}~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , respectively. They optimized the adsorp­ tion process as pH 6, $\mathrm{H_{2}P O^{4-}}$ concentration $25\mathrm{\mg\L^{-1}}$ , adsorbent dosage $0.4\ \mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{L}^{-1}$ , contact time $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ , and $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The experimental results under optimized condition showed that the PABMOFs have a proper adsorption amount of $95.24~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . The adsorption data is well-fitted with pseudo-second-order kinetic and the Langmuir isotherm models. Adsorption thermodynamic study revealed the adsorption process was spontaneous. Therefore, the lower temperature was favorable to adsorption. Finally, the Pot experiment revealed that TRSRFs (P-laden PABMOFs) could endorse the growth of the plant and enhance the uti­ lization efficiency of fertilizer that originated from its proper slowrelease and temperature-responsive behaviors.
## 5.6. Removal of fluoride
The fluoride in drinking water has been reflected in worldwide ecological troubles and has received wide attention in recent decades. The excessive fluoride intake from water causes fluorosis. Fluoride is thus considered beneficial in drinking water at about $0.7~\mathrm{mg~L}^{-1}$ but harmful once it exceeds $1.5\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ , which is the WHO limit being fol­ lowed in most countries [178,179]. Thus, the complete removal or reduction of fluoride from water has received a great deal of interest.
Njaramba et al. [180] fabricated novel MOFs-Alg composite beads, denoted as CA-MIL-53-AC, using Alg (gelling agent) and calcium chlo­ ride (complexing agent) a one-pot and dropping method for removal of fluoride ions from aqueous solution. The developed CA-MIL-53-AC hydrogel displayed an excellent adsorption capacity of $3.18~\mathrm{mg~g^{-1}}$ in optimal condition (pH 7, temperature $313\ \mathrm{K},$ initial concentration 20 $\mathbf{mg\L}^{-1}$ , and adsorbent dosage $\phantom{-}0.024\ g)$ . They suggested that hydro­ phobic, $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions, ion exchange, surface complexation, and hydrogen bonding effects are the most important reasons for the adsorption of fluoride onto/into the CA-MIL-53-AC composite. Also, adsorption and kinetic models were best fitted with Freundlich isotherm and pseudo-first-order, respectively.
