## 6. Conclusions and perspectives
Population growth, industrialization, and climate change lead to negative effects on water resources. The discharge of different pollutants into water bodies leads to risks to human health, animals, and the environment. Amongst, dyes, organic compounds, phosphate, heavy metals, and antibiotics are the most common pollutants that may lead to serious environmental as well as health problems. Numerous strategies, including adsorption, advanced oxidation process, membrane filtration, coagulation, electrochemical, and biological have been applied for the treatment of wastewater containing pollutants as mentioned the above.
Amongst them, the adsorption technology is a promising process for the removal of various pollutants from aqueous media, because of its low cost, selectivity, high efficiency, facile operation, and regeneration po­ tential of the adsorbents. In this context, MOFs-based hydrogels can be considered as potential adsorbents owing to their charming structural, flexible, and excellent physicochemical properties. They have both ad­ vantages of hydrogels and MOFs. Therefore, they have environmental resistances and improvements of processability as well as practical ap­ plications in various areas compared to neat MOFs powders. MOFsbased hydrogels are generally fabricated by incorporating presynthesized MOFs into the hydrogel matrixes or in situ growth of MOFs in the hydrogel matrix.
Though MOFs-based hydrogels had wide attention recently, this area of research has not yet reached the stage of maturity. The stability and re-usability of adsorbents are key points for practical application but not enough has been examined in this area. The stability of MOFs material in MOFs-based hydrogels is a vital point that has been addressed because of the rigidity of MOFs in the processing conditions. MOFs materials in hydrogels must maintain a high surface area, ordered porous structure, and availability. Consequently, to confirm MOF's stability after incor­ poration into a hydrogel matrix, these materials should be characterized using Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR), X-ray diffraction (XRD), and $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ adsorption-desorption isotherm.
In terms of MOFs-based hydrogels, examples of MOFs@naturally occurring derivatives hydrogels, MOFs@graphene hydrogels, etc., are reported. All of these compounds and their preparing strategies are applied in synthesizing different MOFs-based hydrogels, which caused improvement of stability, flexibility, accessibility, and facile handling and for escalating the field of many potential applications. Moreover, these fabrication strategies make available guidance for preparation of other MOFs-based hydrogels generated from unknown MOFs. Thus, further investigation of broadening the range of sustainable and novel matrixes and progressive MOFs-based hydrogels is highly appropriate.
MOFs-based hydrogels are still in the early steps compared to other MOFs-based composites, including enzyme@MOFs and metal@MOFs composites. Therefore, more research is required to design and develop more efficient MOFs-based hydrogels to extend the application ranges of these materials. The high porosity, large size, and fabrication process of MOFs-based hydrogels with desirable stability imply their remarkable potential in commercial production. Some cost-effective and green ap­ proaches for preparing matrixes of MOFs-based hydrogels must be developed with significant quality. When reasonable MOFs-based hydrogels are employed on large scales for practical application, it is a qualitative jump in the expansion of MOFs materials. The variety of MOFs and matrixes in MOFs-based hydrogels and their adaptability provide limitless potential for the regulation of the used properties of these materials.
As discussed above, most MOFs-based hydrogels in environmental science are focused on natural polymers (e.g., alginate, cellulose, gelatin, and starch). In the author's opinion, the combination of natural and biodegradable synthetic polymers can lead to the fabrication of more efficient MOFs-based hydrogels as adsorbents owing to higher me­ chanical and environmental stability as well as lower production cost. In addition, the adsorbent can be post- and/or pre-modified to tune some physicochemical features to improve performance.
The adsorption performance of the MOFs-based hydrogel adsorbents is higher than that of conventional commercial adsorbents. The adapt­ ability in the characteristics of MOFs materials can be created by simple modification or functionalization that allows for extensive applicability of these materials. MOFs, are mainly fabricated by solvothermal ap­ proaches, in which the cost and recovery of organic solvents become the chief hurdle for large-scale production. The reagents usually involve metal sources, organic linkers, solvents for reactions, and solvents for exchange processes. The keys to large-scale production of MOFs, are to substitute the synthesized organic linkers with some raw materials in petroleum and adopt new synthesis and activation procedures to mini­ mize the usage of expensive solvents throughout the processes.
Statistical or computational approaches could be recognized as powerful and effective tools for experimental design and predict the interactions between the adsorbate and adsorbent as well as optimiza­ tion of different parameters, such as reaction time, pH, adsorbent dosage, initial contaminant concentration, and reaction temperature toward the higher performance. According to this review, it can be claimed that MOFs-based hydrogels are proper adsorbents for the removal of various pollutants from aqueous media. However, these materials are under-exploited in the field. In addition, these composite materials have great potential for broad applications in the future.
